.\" tsort.1 -- maux's tsort(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" env.1/man.1.
.TH TSORT 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
tsort \- topological sort
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tsort
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tsort
reads pairs of whitespace/newline\-separated tokens from
.I file
(standard input if none is given): a pair
.RI "\(lq a b \(rq"
means
.I a
must precede
.I b
in the output.  A pair whose two tokens are IDENTICAL
.RI "(\(lq a a \(rq)"
declares the node
.I a
without any ordering edge \(em the same idiom
.BR tsort (1)
implementations have always used for an isolated node.
.PP
.I Tsort
writes one node per line, in an order consistent with every
edge given.  Among nodes with no remaining predecessor at a
given step, this implementation always picks the one that
appeared FIRST in the input (GNU's own tie\-break; BSD's is a
different, unspecified order \(em there is no common oracle for
this choice, so any row that depends on it is fixed by this
tree's own behaviour, not a captured host run).
.PP
An
.B odd
number of tokens is an error: no ordering can be formed from an
unpaired trailing token, so nothing is sorted and
.I tsort
exits 1 without printing an order at all.
.PP
A
.B cycle
(no node ever becomes free) is reported to standard error as
.RI "\(lq tsort: cycle in data \(rq"
plus the still\-unresolved node names, and the sort continues
anyway by forcing the output of the first such node in table
order \(em breaking whichever edge pointed into it, the same
"one edge of the cycle broken" contract
.BR tsort (1)
has always had.  The exit status is 1 if this ever happened.
.SH FILES
.br
/usr/man/TSORT  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
make(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every token paired off and no cycle was
found; 1 if the input held an odd number of tokens, or a cycle
was found (an order is still printed); 2 for a usage error (more
than one file operand).
.SH BUGS
Capacity is fixed at build time (32 distinct tokens, 64 edges,
15 characters per token) \(em a graph past that silently reuses
the last slot rather than growing. A detected cycle's own
reported member list is whatever remains UNRESOLVED at the
moment of detection, which is not necessarily the minimal cycle
in a larger tangled graph.
